# Code owners are requested for review automatically on every pull request.
#
# Owner: Phillip Spies <pspies@amazon.com>
#
# The GitHub handle is what GitHub resolves. An email address only works when it
# is verified on the account AND that account has write access to the repository,
# and an owner GitHub cannot resolve silently produces no review request at all --
# worse than having no CODEOWNERS file. The email above is contact information,
# not the matching rule.
#
# When a maintainer team exists, replace @phspies with the team handle
# (e.g. @awslabs/tolap-maintainers) rather than adding a second individual: a team
# survives someone changing roles, an individual does not.
#
# PATTERN NOTE: these use gitignore-style matching, and a pattern that matches
# nothing fails silently -- it does not error, it just never requests a review. The
# three SDKs do not share a directory shape (Python is
# sdk/python/tolap-core/tolap_core/, TypeScript is
# sdk/typescript/packages/core/src/, .NET is sdk/dotnet/src/Tolap.Core/), so the
# entries below use leading `**/` to match on filename at any depth rather than
# assuming a common layout. Verify with `git check-ignore -v` style reasoning, or
# by confirming `git ls-files` lists the files a pattern is meant to cover.

* @phspies

# --- Security-critical surfaces -------------------------------------------------
#
# Listed explicitly so they keep an owner if the catch-all above is ever narrowed,
# and so a second security reviewer can be added to these lines alone rather than
# to the whole repository. These are the files where a mistake is a data-disclosure
# bug rather than a defect: the normative specs, the cross-SDK signing anchors, and
# the code deciding what a policy permits.

/docs/canonical-enforcement-spec.md   @phspies
/docs/connector-spec.md               @phspies
/schema/                              @phspies
/fixtures/signing/                    @phspies
/fixtures/enforcement/                @phspies
/fixtures/merge-scenarios/            @phspies

# Signing and canonicalization: the bytes that make a policy tamper-evident, and
# the one place all three SDKs must agree exactly.
**/SecurityContextSigner.cs           @phspies
**/CanonicalJson.cs                   @phspies
**/context.py                         @phspies
**/context.ts                         @phspies

# Policy resolution, merging, and enforcement: what a policy actually permits.
**/merger.py                          @phspies
**/merger.ts                          @phspies
**/PolicyMerger.cs                    @phspies
**/resolution.py                      @phspies
**/resolution.ts                      @phspies
**/PolicyResolutionEngine.cs           @phspies
**/enforcement.py                     @phspies
**/enforcement.ts                     @phspies
**/EnforcementEngine.cs               @phspies

# Identity extraction: turns a credential into the principal a policy resolves for.
**/extractors.py                      @phspies
**/extractors.ts                      @phspies
**/JwtIdentityExtractor.cs            @phspies

# SQL pushdown: builds SQL text, so an escaping or dialect mistake is an injection
